<section id="architecture"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Architecture</span><h2>A layer beneath the apps you use</h2></div><div class="architecture-grid"><p class="intro">Relay proposes a layer that sits underneath the applications people use day to day. A person's identity, records, relationships and permissions live at this layer, and applications interact with them according to authority the person grants — rather than owning that presence outright.</p><figure class="diagram-figure"><div class="diagram" aria-hidden="true"><div class="node">YOU</div><div class="connector">↓</div><div class="node">RELAY IDENTITY</div><div class="connector">↓</div><div class="node">REPOSITORY</div><div class="connector">↓</div><div class="node-row"><div class="node small">RECORDS</div><div class="node small">RELATIONSHIPS</div><div class="node small">PERMISSIONS</div></div><div class="connector">↕</div><div class="node">APPLICATIONS</div></div><figcaption>A person holds a Relay identity, which resolves to a repository containing their records, relationships and permissions. Applications read from and write to that layer, within the bounds of what they've been permitted to do. This diagram illustrates the conceptual relationship between these parts — it isn't the complete technical architecture.</figcaption></figure><div class="example"><h3>Why this matters</h3><p>Someone builds a profile, creates records and forms relationships while using Application A. Later, they'd rather use Application B.</p><p>In today's application-centric model, that usually means a new account and rebuilding some or all of that digital presence from scratch. Relay explores a different model: Application B could interact with the same underlying Relay identity, records and relationships, subject to permissions the person grants — no rebuild required.</p><p class="caveat">This is an architectural goal of the protocol, not a claim that this behavior is already implemented.</p></div></div></div></section>
<style>.architecture-grid{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:3rem}.intro,.example p{max-width:38rem}.diagram-figure{margin:0}.diagram{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0;padding:2.5rem 1rem;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius)}.node{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.9375rem;color:var(--text);background:var(--surface-raised);border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:.75rem 1.5rem;text-align:center}.node-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;width:100%;max-width:30rem}.node.small{font-size:.8125rem;padding:.65rem .5rem;color:var(--accent-strong);border-color:var(--accent-dim)}.connector{font-family:var(--font-mono);color:var(--text-faint);font-size:1.125rem;line-height:2}figcaption{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.8125rem;color:var(--text-faint);max-width:38rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:1rem}.example{max-width:38rem}.example h3{margin-bottom:.75rem}.caveat{color:var(--text-faint);font-size:.9375rem}@media(max-width:26rem){.node-row{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.5rem;max-width:16rem}}</style>
